WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:02.535
-Our next guest is
an Emmy-nominated writer

00:00:02.535 --> 00:00:04.637
and comedian you know
from his work on shows like

00:00:04.637 --> 00:00:06.606
"Saturday Night Live"
and "Los Espookys."

00:00:06.606 --> 00:00:09.775
His picture book "I Want To Be
a Vase" is out June 7th.

00:00:09.775 --> 00:00:12.912
Please welcome back to the show
our friend Julio Torres.

00:00:12.912 --> 00:00:14.847
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:14.847 --> 00:00:23.589 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:23.589 --> 00:00:25.091
Julio, how are you?

00:00:25.091 --> 00:00:26.292
-I'm good. How are you?

00:00:26.292 --> 00:00:28.828
-I'm so happy
you wrote this book.

00:00:28.828 --> 00:00:32.598
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:32.598 --> 00:00:34.033
-I don't want to miss anyone.

00:00:34.033 --> 00:00:37.136
-You've described this as
a coffee table book for kids,

00:00:37.136 --> 00:00:39.739
which I think is a lovely -- a
lovely -- a thing that kids --

00:00:39.739 --> 00:00:41.074
Nobody has that for kids yet.

00:00:41.074 --> 00:00:42.509
[ Laughter ]

00:00:42.509 --> 00:00:46.412
-Yeah, like, sometimes I go to
living rooms and, you know,

00:00:46.412 --> 00:00:48.481
they have their coffee table
books on, like, seashells

00:00:48.481 --> 00:00:49.882
and a Madonna coffee table book,

00:00:49.882 --> 00:00:51.717
but they don't have
one of these.

00:00:51.717 --> 00:00:54.320
-Yeah, this is great.

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:57.189
So, inanimate objects are
the characters in the book.

00:00:57.189 --> 00:01:00.627
You have a plunger,
a vacuum cleaner.

00:01:00.627 --> 00:01:02.495
And the plunger
wants to be a vase.

00:01:02.495 --> 00:01:03.863
What made you want to write
a kids book

00:01:03.863 --> 00:01:06.432
where the characters
were objects?

00:01:06.432 --> 00:01:09.335
-Well, I don't know. I wanted to
find something unusual.

00:01:09.335 --> 00:01:12.605
I didn't want it -- I didn't
want to write a book about,

00:01:12.605 --> 00:01:15.241
like, a little bear or like --
-Okay.

00:01:15.241 --> 00:01:20.846
[ Laughter ]

00:01:20.846 --> 00:01:24.083
-I don't know if you know.
I just -- I just did that.

00:01:24.083 --> 00:01:26.452
[ Laughter ]

00:01:26.452 --> 00:01:30.323
-Which...
Which is why -- like,

00:01:30.323 --> 00:01:33.225
'cause the bear-book bar
was set so high.

00:01:33.225 --> 00:01:35.361
-Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:35.361 --> 00:01:40.967
-So, I felt like, you know,
pick -- pick your track.

00:01:40.967 --> 00:01:43.636
-Yeah.
-Yeah, build your own.

00:01:43.636 --> 00:01:46.772
I'm not gonna have like, what,
"Late Night with Julio Torres"?

00:01:46.772 --> 00:01:49.108
No. You know what I mean?
[ Laughter ]

00:01:49.108 --> 00:01:51.244
-Exactly. I think why we're such
nice friends is

00:01:51.244 --> 00:01:52.912
we're on different --
we're on parallel tracks,

00:01:52.912 --> 00:01:55.181
not the same track.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:01:55.181 --> 00:01:56.982
-It's really cool
that you did that. Thank you.

00:01:56.982 --> 00:01:59.118
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:59.118 --> 00:02:03.589
A friendship in TV makeup
90% of the time.

00:02:03.589 --> 00:02:06.359
-Yeah, exactly.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:06.359 --> 00:02:07.927
I think you can all attest
that we're friends

00:02:07.927 --> 00:02:09.395
'cause you've seen
most of our interactions.

00:02:09.395 --> 00:02:11.697
[ Laughter ]
-Exactly, yeah, yeah.

00:02:11.697 --> 00:02:14.667
-But why did you choose to make
a plunger your hero?

00:02:14.667 --> 00:02:17.770
-Well, you know, I was trying to
think of what

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:21.140
the scrappiest object would be,

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:24.676
and I was thinking about
the hierarchy of objects.

00:02:24.676 --> 00:02:27.813
And if you are a plunger,

00:02:27.813 --> 00:02:30.516
it's safe to say you're
at the bottom of the totem pole.

00:02:30.516 --> 00:02:32.151
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:32.151 --> 00:02:36.122
-Because no one -- no one's
happy to be using a plunger.

00:02:36.122 --> 00:02:37.957
[ Laughter ]

00:02:37.957 --> 00:02:40.660
Which is why most of them are so
poorly designed, I feel like,

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:43.462
'cause everyone's just like...
[ Laughter ]

00:02:43.462 --> 00:02:47.900
No one's excited that they're
gonna use a plunger.

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:50.002
-No one's looking at it
while they're using it even.

00:02:50.002 --> 00:02:52.305
-No, they avert their eyes.
-You'd see the design flaws

00:02:52.305 --> 00:02:55.341
more, but you're like,
you're right.

00:02:55.341 --> 00:02:58.745
-Imagine having a job
where people were like...

00:02:58.745 --> 00:03:00.980
[ Laughter ]

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:03.149
"Just get it over with."

00:03:03.149 --> 00:03:06.753
-And then the villain
is a vacuum cleaner.

00:03:06.753 --> 00:03:08.888
I mean, as much as an object
can be a villain.

00:03:08.888 --> 00:03:11.423
But the vacuum cleaner sort of
believes you should not

00:03:11.423 --> 00:03:13.259
want to aspire to be
a different object.

00:03:13.259 --> 00:03:15.061
-Well, yeah.

00:03:15.061 --> 00:03:19.465
I don't think that Vacuum
is a villain so much

00:03:19.465 --> 00:03:24.103
as Vacuum is trapped in a cage
of Vacuum's own making.

00:03:24.103 --> 00:03:26.605
[ Laughter ]

00:03:26.605 --> 00:03:29.642
I don't -- I don't think that
Vacuum is like,

00:03:29.642 --> 00:03:31.077
"Oh, I'm gonna be villainous."

00:03:31.077 --> 00:03:34.180
I think that Vacuum just, you
know, watches a lot of Fox News.

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:36.649
[ Laughter ]

00:03:36.649 --> 00:03:41.554
But, yeah, Vacuum doesn't like
the idea that objects can be

00:03:41.554 --> 00:03:46.492
whomever objects choose to be.

00:03:46.492 --> 00:03:48.661
And again, I was thinking, it
should be a vacuum

00:03:48.661 --> 00:03:50.562
because it's like,
if you are a vacuum --

00:03:50.562 --> 00:03:52.264
if you're an object
and you're a vacuum,

00:03:52.264 --> 00:03:56.035
that is one of the most
cutthroat object industries

00:03:56.035 --> 00:03:59.505
there is because it's like,
if you're a vacuum cleaner,

00:03:59.505 --> 00:04:02.375
you're constantly worried
you're gonna get replaced

00:04:02.375 --> 00:04:07.647
'cause there's always a faster,
better vacuum cleaner out there.

00:04:07.647 --> 00:04:10.149
-Yeah. Right, if you let it
slide just a little bit --

00:04:10.149 --> 00:04:13.086
-If you let it slide just a
little bit, you're out of there.

00:04:13.086 --> 00:04:14.487
[ Laughter ]

00:04:14.487 --> 00:04:18.191
It's sort of like a -- sort of
like Wall Street people.

00:04:18.191 --> 00:04:21.126
-Yeah.
-Or like corporate people who

00:04:21.126 --> 00:04:26.699
are, like,
"Well, I wake up at 5:00

00:04:26.699 --> 00:04:32.571
and go to Peloton and then I go
to work, and if you don't

00:04:32.571 --> 00:04:34.841
make as much money as you do,
that's your problem."

00:04:34.841 --> 00:04:36.108
[ Laughter ]

00:04:36.108 --> 00:04:38.411
-That's a vacuum, yeah.
-Yeah, that's a vacuum.

00:04:38.411 --> 00:04:40.045
[ Laughter ]

00:04:40.045 --> 00:04:44.216
It's very, like, okay, like,
your life is all efficiency.

00:04:44.216 --> 00:04:47.619
That's great for you.

00:04:47.619 --> 00:04:49.755
[ Laughter ]
But some of us want to be vases.

00:04:49.755 --> 00:04:51.290
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:04:51.290 --> 00:04:52.759
-It really is a beautiful story.

00:04:52.759 --> 00:04:54.460
I can't wait to read it
to my kids.

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:57.096
I also want to congratulate you.
I know you're very busy.

00:04:57.096 --> 00:04:59.231
And I'm happy you did this
because, you know, what you were

00:04:59.231 --> 00:05:01.100
just talking about --
sometimes people are too busy

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:04.537
and they don't find other people
to help them out,

00:05:04.537 --> 00:05:07.974
but you just hired
a social-media manager.

00:05:07.974 --> 00:05:09.908
-Y-Y-Y-Yeah.

00:05:09.908 --> 00:05:12.144
-Okay.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:12.144 --> 00:05:14.947
I guess they have been posting
a fair amount on your socials

00:05:14.947 --> 00:05:16.916
to let people know they --
-Right.

00:05:16.916 --> 00:05:21.220
I -- I --
I'm giving her a chance.

00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:22.788
-Okay.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:22.788 --> 00:05:29.128
-She's -- She's struggling
a little bit, but yeah.

00:05:29.128 --> 00:05:30.930
-Is her name Piralina Pina-Pina?

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:33.365
-No, her name is Pirulinpinpina.

00:05:33.365 --> 00:05:34.834
-Okay.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:34.834 --> 00:05:38.204
Pirulin-- okay, Pirulinpinpina.
Yes, and here she is.

00:05:38.204 --> 00:05:39.906
This is a photo of herself
she just posted.

00:05:39.906 --> 00:05:41.373
[ Laughter ]

00:05:41.373 --> 00:05:42.975
Can I read what she just said?

00:05:42.975 --> 00:05:45.778
Again, I get why you're maybe
a little frustrated with her.

00:05:45.778 --> 00:05:48.881
She wrote, "Let's get
this Monday right."

00:05:48.881 --> 00:05:50.349
-It's not Monday.
-Right.

00:05:50.349 --> 00:05:52.251
[ Laughter ]

00:05:52.251 --> 00:05:53.686
Yeah, so, right off the bat --

00:05:53.686 --> 00:05:55.487
-So, like, the attention to
detail already.

00:05:55.487 --> 00:05:58.557
-Yeah. And also the hope that
she wants to get it right,

00:05:58.557 --> 00:06:00.525
and right away it's wrong.
That's bad.

00:06:00.525 --> 00:06:02.594
"Let's get this Monday right."
Right off the bat, wrong.

00:06:02.594 --> 00:06:03.930
"I am Pirulinpinpina.

00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:07.066
Yes, and I manage socials
for Julio currently.

00:06:07.066 --> 00:06:10.202
In this post, you will find much
product for you to consume.

00:06:10.202 --> 00:06:12.338
Much love to little consumers.
Be happy."

00:06:12.338 --> 00:06:14.006
That's bad, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:14.006 --> 00:06:15.808
-Yeah.

00:06:15.808 --> 00:06:22.414
She calls my "fans"
"little consumers."

00:06:22.414 --> 00:06:23.749
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:23.749 --> 00:06:27.052
-Which there's a beautiful
honesty about that, right?

00:06:27.052 --> 00:06:28.587
'Cause it's like she's like --

00:06:28.587 --> 00:06:30.055
-Yeah, they're just product,
right?

00:06:30.055 --> 00:06:32.858
-Let's not pretend that we don't
live in a capitalist society

00:06:32.858 --> 00:06:35.094
where art is product, right?
-Yeah, yeah.

00:06:35.094 --> 00:06:37.563
-No, it's like, "Oh, you wrote
a -- you made product."

00:06:37.563 --> 00:06:39.131
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:39.131 --> 00:06:42.067
-So, it must be nice because
that allows you to maybe be

00:06:42.067 --> 00:06:44.202
a little bit more artistic,
knowing that she's going to

00:06:44.202 --> 00:06:46.138
really drill down and try to
sell your product.

00:06:46.138 --> 00:06:48.941
-Well, the problem is then
friends are like,

00:06:48.941 --> 00:06:50.576
"Did you see
what she just posted?

00:06:50.576 --> 00:06:52.511
She should take it down."
-Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:06:52.511 --> 00:06:54.680
That's stressful.
-And then I'm like,

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:56.949
"Girl, why are you commenting
about war?"

00:06:56.949 --> 00:06:59.918
[ Laughter ]
"Take it down."

00:06:59.918 --> 00:07:02.855
But yeah,
she's very overwhelmed.

00:07:02.855 --> 00:07:04.356 line:0%
She's like this big,
so I understand

00:07:04.356 --> 00:07:05.825 line:0%
why she would be overwhelmed.

00:07:05.825 --> 00:07:07.560
-I mean, she does look like
someone who's pretending

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:10.228
not to be overwhelmed,
but you can -- I feel like

00:07:10.228 --> 00:07:13.199
if you have a good eye for
expression, you're like,

00:07:13.199 --> 00:07:15.334
"Oh, she's over her head."
-She's, like, always manic.

00:07:15.334 --> 00:07:17.169
And, like, you'd think
that, like,

00:07:17.169 --> 00:07:19.972
"Oh, Julio's gonna be on 'Seth,'
he has a book to promote,"

00:07:19.972 --> 00:07:22.207
you'd think that she'd be here,
but then she was like,

00:07:22.207 --> 00:07:26.112
"Oh, my cousin's wedding,
it happens to be that day."

00:07:26.112 --> 00:07:27.880
[ Laughter ]

00:07:27.880 --> 00:07:31.217
And, I'm like, "Okay," and then
she just texted me saying,

00:07:31.217 --> 00:07:34.453
"My bad, my cousin
is already married."

00:07:34.453 --> 00:07:36.889
[ Laughter ]

00:07:36.889 --> 00:07:40.292
"Can I still come?" And I'm
like, no. No. No, don't come.

00:07:40.292 --> 00:07:42.361
-She forgot her cousin
was married?

00:07:42.361 --> 00:07:45.965
-She was like --
I think that tomorrow

00:07:45.965 --> 00:07:47.767
she'll realize
whose wedding it was.

00:07:47.767 --> 00:07:49.468
-Oh, I see.
So there was a wedding.

00:07:49.468 --> 00:07:50.802
-I think there was "a" wedding.

00:07:50.802 --> 00:07:52.138
I think she maybe, like --

00:07:52.138 --> 00:07:54.306
She probably doesn't even
know them.

00:07:54.306 --> 00:07:58.177
-She just read a wedding
announcement somewhere.

00:07:58.177 --> 00:08:00.179
This is -- I'm sorry about this.

00:08:00.179 --> 00:08:01.614
I'm so happy about the book,

00:08:01.614 --> 00:08:03.015
and it's really, really nice
to have you back on the show.

00:08:03.015 --> 00:08:04.383
Thank you so much.
-Thank you for having me.

00:08:04.383 --> 00:08:07.886
-You guys, that's Julio Torres.
"I Want To Be a Vase."

00:08:07.886 --> 00:08:09.721
Here it is one more time.
It's out June 7th.

00:08:09.721 --> 00:08:10.790
We'll be right back with

00:08:10.790 --> 00:08:12.024
a performance from
"Six the Musical."

